welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Erin Jareau professor of film studies  and East Asian languages and  literature's at Yale University  professor Jareau teaches courses in  Japanese cinema and popular culture film  studies and film genre as well as  seminars on Japanese film and cultural  theory he has published numerous  articles and books in English Japanese  and other languages on such topics as  Japanese early cinema film theory  contemporary directors genre censorship  and cinematic representations of  minorities today we talk with him about  the history of Japanese film theory  welcome professor Jerrod uh-huh thank  you very much for having me  so you are in the process of writing a  book on the history of Japanese film  theory yes what led you to undertake  that endeavor well I think it's a rather  esoteric topic usually when I try to  explain it to people they kind of  scratch their heads at first  uh-huh especially for people not  familiar with film studies as a whole  they wonder what is film theory well in  some ways it's it's the most basic  question about well what is cinema what  defines cinema in comparison to the  other arts for instance those were some  of the very basic questions asked in  film theory at the beginning I has  progressed beyond that so for instance  later on questions of well does cinema  have its own language is cinema a  language how does cinema transmit  meaning how does meaning or  signification occur within cinema so  there are many many many different very  fundamental questions about cinema much  of the questions have evolved around for  instance the issue of modernity  if cinema is a modern medium what does  cinema tell us about modernity  what does modernity tell us about cinema  and unfortunately while in film studies  you know film theory is obviously one of  the central aspects of the discipline  when you open a book giving you a you  know the history of film theory or some  of the anthologies of the great writers  in film theory you never find anyone  who's not an American or European okay  that might be because no one else wrote  about it but it's not the case I mean  especially when you look at Japan Japan  has a very long history in fact there  were people who were writing books about  cinema from a very philosophical or  theoretical perspective even before they  were doing it in Europe so cinema has  really have has had a very very vibrant  history in Japan that has been  accompanied by a theoretical endeavor  but that history has never been told and  that's where you come that's right yes  when did Japan start doing cinema when  do they begin making films well in  general most people date cinema to 1895  which is when you had the first public  performance of a projected film and that  occurred in France with the Lumiere  brothers and what was interesting about  the Lumiere brothers is that they  actually developed a camera that could  also be a projector Wow  and they sent cameramen around the world  because then they could film things and  then project them and so as early as  1896 97-98 you already have people in  Japan projecting films so in some ways  film history begins in Japan at that  time but that was mostly foreign  cameramen then it's really past the year  1900 that you start seeing more Japanese  but especially the film industry in  Japan really really gets going around  1907 1908 but it's in 1914 that you  already have a wonderful book written by  this fellow named Juan de Asano sky 430  pages about cinema a lot of it's about  the technical aspects but part of it is  also he's really interested in how  cinema  is defining this new thing called  modernity mm-hmm  it's a really fascinating work and it's  before most of what was most of what is  canonized as like the first books on  film theory in the West okay and so what  will some of the issues be that you'll  tackle in your book well one of the  issues is this whole problem about the  kind of division that has been created  in the history of film theory about how  it is been concentrated on the West as  if in some ways only the West does film  theory right now that's simply not a  problem with the West in some ways that  has also been something that Japanese  thinkers have for one reason or another  recognized so even when you open books  in Japan about the history of film  theory you don't find Japanese names  continue why do you think that is well a  part of it is that even though of course  Japan was never colonized by the West  even though you could talk a little bit  about what the occupation after World  War two really meant nonetheless Japan  wasn't is any kind of neo-colonial  perspective in that especially as Japan  opened up to the West Western knowledge  was considered superior to Japanese  knowledge and you have many  intellectuals not only in film but in  many fields who for one reason or  another are always experiencing a kind  of inferiority complex that somehow what  we are doing in intellectual endeavor is  never quite enough to equal what the  West is doing and that actually was one  reason that Japanese film theory is  actually quite interesting quite  different because you have these people  who are actually writing extremely  brilliant things about cinema in fact  broaching certain ideas before people in  the West are approaching those ideas but  they're always doing it with the  consciousness that what they're doing is  not going to be recognized by the  West and it's that kind of complex in  the multiple senses of that term is  because a very complex but it's also a  kind of like inferiority complex  that has led Japanese film theory to  always be an investigation of theory as  well it's a kind of meta level to it  which I think has made it a very very  interesting theory especially today  because as film has essentially  disappeared it's now the digital age  films are no longer made on celluloid  that has created a crisis for film  studies well because you know so much of  our discipline has been centered around  this photographic process okay of  celluloid with an emulsion receiving  light and in some ways becoming an  imprint of reality but now things are  made undal to the point that sometimes  you can see images which look real but  we're completely made inside the  computer right  that's just one of the many examples of  the problems that the digital has  created so in some ways film studies is  rethinking its discipline as well as  rethinking its history and actually part  of that process is rethinking film  theory there is actually an organization  a worldwide organization called the  permanent seminar on the histories of  film theories which is actively looking  at these film theories that have been  ignored up until now in fact starting  today the next session of the permanent  seminar is going to be having a  conference at Yale oh great over the  next couple days and I'll be  participating just a little bit there  and will you be talking about jab you  know things going on in Japan I will be  obnoxious and insist on okay um you know  I am curious you are a westerner okay  and you're writing in this book about  you know the history of Japanese film  theory are well that you being Wes  well that have any kind of role and and  your perspective in the book well it's  always a a issue that I have to deal  with as a scholar from the West but in  some ways it's always been an issue that  I've dealt with because I actually lived  in Japan for 12 years before coming to  Yale and I was very active writing many  of my articles in Japanese sometimes I  felt that my position was not in Japan  or in America it was somewhere in the  middle of the Pacific Ocean probably  drowning somewhere but that I always had  this kind of ambiguous position and it's  one that I've thought is actually kind  of productive that I can actually use  that position both to provide to provide  an outside perspective a critical as  perspective not only on Japanese cinema  but also on Western theory and you know  it's a good opportunity I think to do  some interesting things because frankly  most scholars in Japan have not thought  about Japanese film theory there's only  one book that's been written about it by  a great film critic named Sato Adele has  a very eclectic book it's basically him  picking a couple people who've written  about cinema but most of the time just  talking about what he wants to talk  about it's still a very interesting book  but it's not really a good history but I  still run into many Japanese who say  what there was film theory in Japan I  mean that's the kind of complex that I'm  dealing with so in fact it's actually  good for me to be a westerner saying  well actually there's actually quite a  lot there and already some of the things  I'm presenting both in Japanese and  English are causing a lot of other  people to start thinking about this so  one of my projects beyond writing this  history is actually to do an anthology  okay  but what's interesting is that we've  decided to do an anthology  both in Japanese and in English because  nothing has actually been produced in  Japan  on that and it'll be myself another  American scholar and a Japanese scholar  who are the three editors so it's very  much an international endeavor and  hopefully it'll be an intervention not  only in Japanese film studies but also  in American or European film studies you  know I was going to ask you how will  your book be unique from the others and  basically you've already answer the  question there are no sadder there been  some great works in Japanese film  history or Japanese film studies which  deal with particular periods or in  particular issues which within that  might take up some of these theorists  about you know this one is really going  to be the first one that tries to deal  with the longer history from supposedly  the beginning towards up to today or  well that's the big problem of writing  this because you know it helps when  other people have done it before because  they can choose or select what's the  important thing to talk about  whereas with no other people writing  about it I've got to do that so it means  you know I've got to go through  literally thousands of articles and try  to figure out well what's important what  are the things that deserve being talked  about in my history where do you start  where do you end who do you include who  do you not include and there's a lot of  politics involved in that which can be  rather nasty and rather complex I will  probably be criticized by lots and lots  of graduate students in the future about  my selections but nonetheless I think  it's very exciting because there's a lot  of new ground to break and kind of feel  like a little bit of a pioneer there you  know and part of what I've decided to do  at least about when to end  it is to probably end around 1980 and  why there one reason being because you  know really one of the great last film  theorists Hassoun me shigeko that's  really when he becomes very popular so I  might end with his work but the other  problem is is that the people who are  writing after that are probably all my  friends okay or colleagues and then you  get into the problem of including and  not including being a very personal  issue I say so I kind of don't want to  step on too many people's toes so I'll  kind of arbitrarily kind of but I think  with some good reasons ended around 1980  okay now does your research involve  actually viewing any films well I mean  that's one of the interesting things  about the Japanese history of film  theory because especially if these  Japanese theorists were thinking about  cinema in fundamental ways but still  working under a kind of oppressive  condition where philosophy or thought or  theory is a Western phenomenon Japanese  are not supposed to be doing it it  sometimes has led Japanese to actually  engage in other forms of theory doing  questions or pursuing questions of what  is cinema in fields not normally  recognised as theory so so for instance  in film criticism usually in the West we  clearly divide film criticism in film  theory but in my case for instance one  interesting thing is that there are  actually many pieces of literature which  are actually very very profound  investigations of film but also I think  you can make an argument there are a  number of films themselves that are  actually very very intriguing  investigations of what is cinema in part  that comes from the fact that some of  the great film theorists in Japan are  actually some of the great film makers  okay I mean you see this in Europe as  well less in America  but you know many filmmakers are  actually quite intellectual and quite  profound in thinking about their own  discipline now you could argue that all  filmmakers in some ways are trying to  think about what you can do in cinema  but especially in Japan and in some  places in Europe some of them actually  wrote that down on paper and so you can  look at those theorists and also at  their films and really try to think  about well what are they really saying  about cinema not simply in their words  but also in their images so you know I'm  not sure how far along you are on the  process with the book but for better or  worse have you come across anything that  have has surprised you or something that  you haven't expected you know I mean  there are a number of things I mean  obviously you come across some brilliant  people one of the great finds I think  has been this fellow named a guy Mitch  stato who is largely forgotten in Japan  he wrote just really one book plus a  number of essays but one book called  form expression and formation or film  expression in formation which is  brilliant it's really one of the best  books I think written on cinema anywhere  Wow and especially in the 1970s 1980s  one of the great French philosophers  Jill de leurs wrote a book on cinema  which especially talked about the issue  of time and cinema he is one of the  representatives of a trend we call film  philosophy which is sometimes  distinguished from film theory that is  using cinema to answer fundamental  questions about philosophy okay like  what is time but now a guy meets cello  in 1940 is basically talking about what  Deleuze is talking about forty years  before I mean obviously in a different  way but still in a really really  fascinating way so there are these finds  about you know really really interesting  people but also I think you know there  are trends throughout Japanese film  theory which I think are very  fascinating  for instance in American or European  especially British film studies it was  from the 1970s that people really  started thinking about spectatorship how  spectators the people who watch films  are actually very very important in the  process of film production that they in  fact are part of the process of creating  the meaning of films but what's the  interesting is that in Japanese film  history especially this history of film  theory  you see people talking about that from  1914 because that's in fact one of the  main points of Gandia snowscape it's he  actually doesn't talk about individual  films he doesn't talk about directors  because to him it doesn't matter  it's the spectators who make films and  to him that is the reason why cinema is  modern because it is the sign in fact  the representative of the arrival of the  modern masses that they are going to be  taking over culture because it is  through their cinema viewing that  they're creating culture which thing you  cannot do with literature you cannot do  with theatre so cinema to him is the  marker of a new civilization and  precisely centered around the activity  of the spectator and that becomes I  think a trend you see that in other  theorists in the 20s 30s 40s into the  post-war who are very interested in this  issue of spectatorship which has not  been a major issue in Western film  theory necessarily in the same way up  until you know the 1970s well it sounds  like a fascinating project I wish you  all the best and thank you so much for  being here with us and sharing some of  your work well thank you for inviting me  again for more information about  Professor Jareau and his research please  visit our website at yale.edu slash  macmillan report be sure to join us for  again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale

